---
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import Pod from '../../layouts/Pod.astro';

const TABLE = 'https://mtg.subch.us';
const DISCORD = 'https://discord.gg/cGGjXK7Qa5';

// Artist credit comes from the fetch script's output so it cannot drift from
// the images actually on disk.
// Astro builds with cwd at the project root, so a plain relative path is
// steadier here than resolving against import.meta.url.
const credits = JSON.parse(readFileSync('public/mtg/img/credits.json', 'utf8')).credits;
// The five house tribes. They no longer get their own section - they are the
// site's colour identity, painted across the hero as a backdrop.
const TRIBES = [
  { key: 'elf', color: 'G' },
  { key: 'goblin', color: 'R' },
  { key: 'merfolk', color: 'U' },
  { key: 'angel', color: 'W' },
  { key: 'vampire', color: 'B' },
];
---

<Pod title="Goonmander">
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="tribe-strip" aria-hidden="true">
      {TRIBES.map((t) => (
        <span data-c={t.color} style={`background-image:url(/mtg/img/${t.key}.jpg)`}></span>
      ))}
    </div>
    <span class="pips">
      {['W', 'U', 'B', 'R', 'G'].map((c) => <span class={`pip pip-${c}`}>{c}</span>)}
    </span>
    <p class="eyebrow">Four-player EDH, in the browser</p>
    <h1>Goonmander</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Commander with every rule enforced and nothing to install. Open the table,
      pick a deck, sit down.
    </p>

    <div class="hero-actions" style="margin-bottom:1.4rem">
      <span class="status" id="server-status" data-state="unknown">
        <span class="dot" id="status-dot"></span>
        <span><strong id="status-text">Checking…</strong> <small id="status-sub"></small></span>
      </span>
    </div>

    <div class="hero-actions">
      <a class="btn" href={TABLE}>Open the table</a>
      <a class="btn ghost" href="#start">How to join</a>
      <a class="btn ghost" href="/mtg/decks/">Browse all decks</a>
      {DISCORD && <a class="btn ghost" href={DISCORD} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Discord</a>}
    </div>
  </section>


  <section class="section" id="featured">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Ready to play</p>
        <h2>What people are playing</h2>
        <p>The most-viewed decks right now, split by power bracket. Open one to see the list, change it, and export.</p>
      </div>
      <a class="btn ghost" href="/mtg/decks/">See all <span id="all-count"></span> →</a>
    </div>
    <div id="featured-decks"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="start">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">First time?</p>
        <h2>Getting in</h2>
      </div>
    </div>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li class="step">
        <h3>Be on the Discord</h3>
        <p>
          The table is private. It checks that you are a member of our Discord
          server and nothing else — no account to make, no password to remember.
        </p>
        {DISCORD && <p><a class="btn" href={DISCORD} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Join the Discord</a></p>}
      </li>
      <li class="step">
        <h3>Open the table</h3>
        <p>
          Go to <a href={TABLE}><code>mtg.subch.us</code></a> in any desktop
          browser. Accept the terms, pick a nickname, and you are in. Phones are
          too small for a four-player board; a laptop or a big tablet is fine.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="step">
        <h3>Bring a deck</h3>
        <p>
          Grab one from above, or open <strong>My Decks → Build / Import</strong>
          and paste a Moxfield or Archidekt link, or the list itself. Starter
          decks are built in if you just want to play. New sets show up on their
          own, so there is nothing to update.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="step">
        <h3>Sit down</h3>
        <p>
          Open <strong>Multiplayer</strong>, join a <strong>subch table</strong>,
          choose <strong>Commander</strong> and your deck. The host presses start
          when everyone is ready. Short a player? <strong>Add a bot</strong> fills
          the seat. Voice on Discord, as always.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="convert">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Already have a list?</p>
        <h2>Check a decklist</h2>
        <p>
          Paste from Moxfield, Archidekt, or plain text. It checks the count, the
          colour identity, and our house ban list — the table imports the same
          links directly, so this is the pre-flight, not the import.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <form id="convert-form">
      <input type="text" id="deckname" placeholder="Deck name" style="width:100%;max-width:22rem;margin-bottom:0.6rem" />
      <textarea
        id="decklist"
        aria-label="Decklist"
        placeholder={"1 Atraxa, Praetors' Voice\n1 Sol Ring\n1 Arcane Signet\n…\n\nA Commander section or Moxfield *CMDR* markers are understood."}
      ></textarea>
      <div class="hero-actions" style="margin-top:0.6rem">
        <button type="submit">Check the list</button>
        <button type="button" class="ghost" id="convert-clear">Clear</button>
      </div>
    </form>
    <div id="convert-out"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-head"><div><h2>Good to know</h2></div></div>

    <details class="faq">
      <summary>What do the bracket numbers mean?</summary>
      <p>
        Wizards' own power scale. Bracket 2 is precon level, 3 is an upgraded
        deck, 4 is seriously tuned. The number shown is worked out from the
        deck's actual contents — how many cards from the official Game Changers
        list it runs, plus whether it does mass land destruction or chains extra
        turns. Agree a bracket before you shuffle and the evening goes better.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq">
      <summary>Do I need to buy or install anything?</summary>
      <p>
        No. Every card ever printed is in the table, and it runs in the browser.
        That is rather the point.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq">
      <summary>Can I practise on my own?</summary>
      <p>
        Yes. <strong>Play Offline</strong> or a table with three bots gives you
        a full game against the computer with the same rules engine. Good for
        learning a deck before game night.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq">
      <summary>The table says it is down.</summary>
      <p>
        It runs around the clock, so this should be rare. The check runs every
        few minutes and can lag, so try opening it anyway before assuming the
        worst — then shout on Discord.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq">
      <summary>The table rejected my deck.</summary>
      <p>
        Nearly always one of two things: not exactly 100 cards, or a card
        outside your commander's colour identity. Our house ban list (stricter
        than paper — Mana Crypt, Jeweled Lotus, Dockside and Nadu are all out)
        is on the honour system for now; the checker above tells you before
        you sit down.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details class="faq">
      <summary>What happened to XMage?</summary>
      <p>
        Retired. The old server is off and nothing needs downloading anymore. If
        you still have <code>.dck</code> files, paste the list into the deck
        importer and it comes straight across.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <p class="credits" style="margin-top:2.5rem">
    Header art:
    {credits.map((c, i) => (
      <span>{i > 0 ? ' · ' : ''}<a href={c.scryfall} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{c.card}</a> by {c.artist}</span>
    ))}
  </p>

  <script>
    import '../../client/home.js';
  </script>
</Pod>
